    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "A complex number $z$ is a root of the equation $z^{1997} = 0$ if and only if $z$ is an $1997^{th}$ root of $0$, i.e., $z$ is an $1997^{th}$ root of unity, i.e., $z$ is a solution to the equation $z^n = 1$ for some integer $n$.\nThe equation $z^n = 1$ has exactly $n$ solutions in the complex plane, given by $z = e^{i2\\pi k/n}$ for $k = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1$.\nSo, $z^{1997} = 1$ has exactly $1997$ solutions in the complex plane, given by $z = e^{2i\\pi k/1997}$ for $k = 0, 1, \\ldots, 1996$.\nSince $v$ and $w$ are distinct, randomly chosen roots of unity, the set of possible values for $|v+w|$ is the set of possible values for $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^n z_k \\right|$,\nwhere $z_k = e^{i2\\pi k/n}$ for $k = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1$ are a permutation of the $n$ distinct roots.\nBy symmetry, the set of possible values for $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^n z_k \\right|$ is the same whether $n$ is even or odd, so we only need to consider the case where $n$ is even.\nIn this case, $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k + \\sum_{k = n/2 + 1}^{n-1} z_k \\right| = \\left| 2 \\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k \\right|$, which occurs if and only if $\\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k = -\\frac{1}{2}$ or $\\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nWe now need to find the probability that $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k \\right| \\leq \\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}$.\nNote that if $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k \\right| = 1$, then $v$ and $w$ are complex conjugates, so $v = re^{i\\theta}$ and $w = re^{-i\\theta}$ for some real number $r$ and angle $\\theta$.\nThen, $\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}} < 1$ implies that $\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{3}} < 1$ implies $\\theta > 0 \\pmod{2\\pi}$, and so $\\theta \\in \\left[ 0, \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) \\pmod{2\\pi}$,\nand this occurs with probability $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nSince the probability that $v$ and $w$ are complex conjugates is $\\frac{1}{2}$, the probability that $\\left| \\sum_{k = 0}^{n/2} z_k \\right| \\leq \\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}$ is $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{\\pi}{6}",
