      "text": "\\begin{align*} v, w &= e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}n} \\\\ &\\hphantom{{}=e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}{n}}}& & \\text{for }1,2,\\dots,1997 \\\\ &\\hphantom{{}=e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}{n}}}\\median_+ &= e^{\\frac{3\\pi }2n}e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}n} \\end{align*}\nIn general, if $v, w$ are roots of the equation $z^n-1=0,$ then we can express them as the sine and cosine of same base.  Now, work by symmetry:\n\\begin{align*} \\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}&\\le\\left|e^{\\frac{3\\pi }2n}e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}n}\\right| \\\\ &=\\left|e^{\\frac{3\\pi }2n}\\right| \\left|e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}n}\\right| \\\\ &=\\left|e^{\\frac{3\\pi }2n}\\right| \\end{align*}Since\n\\[\\left|e^{\\frac{3\\pi }2n}\\right|\\text{ is a real number and equal to }\\cos \\frac{3\\pi }2n-, \\]\nwe have\n\\[\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}\\le\\cos \\frac{3\\pi }2n-\\]\n\nBy symmetry, this is true for $n\\pmod{1997}=\\left\\{3,7,11,15\\right\\}\\mod{1997}=\\left\\{3,7,11,15\\right\\}.$  We count the number of roots by Lemma \\hyperlink{Lemma}{below}:\n\\[[0,3] + [3,3] + [3,7] + [7,7] + [3,3] + [6,15] + [10,9]+ [7,9]\\plus{}\\le1997\\qquad=1004\\]\\Thus, the probability that $v$ and $w$ satisfy the condition is $\\frac{502}{1997}\\boxed{\\frac{198.5}{1997}}.$",
